CONCEPTS = [
concept("data-catalog", "ar", "Data catalogues and metadata", GS, "I",
  "data catalog catalogue data catalog catalogs metadata technical metadata business glossary lineage discovery datahub openmetadata unity catalog collibra alation ownership",
  "A data catalogue is a searchable inventory of an organisation's data assets with their meaning, owners, quality and lineage, so people can find and trust data.",
  "Technical metadata, such as schemas, columns, types, freshness and usage, is harvested automatically from warehouses, BI tools and pipelines. Business metadata adds descriptions, owners, a business glossary of agreed terms, and classifications such as PII. Lineage shows how data flows from sources through transformations to dashboards, at table or column level, which helps impact analysis and debugging. Popular tools include DataHub, OpenMetadata, Unity Catalog, Collibra and Alation. Catalogues only stay useful when metadata collection is automated and owners keep descriptions current as part of their normal workflow.",
  "Before changing a column in the orders table, an engineer checks column-level lineage in the catalogue and finds it feeds 14 dashboards and one finance extract, then notifies their owners.",
  "Why must most catalogue metadata be collected automatically?",
  "Expect what a catalogue contains, how lineage helps, and how you'd get people to keep it current.",
  ["governance", "data-observability", "pii-handling"]),
concept("access-control", "ar", "Access control: RBAC, ABAC, row and column security", GS, "I",
  "access control rbac abac role based attribute based row level security column level security masking policies least privilege grants tags",
  "Access control decides who can see which data. Roles, attributes, and row and column policies let one dataset safely serve many users.",
  "Role-based access control (RBAC) grants privileges to roles, such as analyst_finance, and users get roles, which is simple to audit. Attribute-based access control (ABAC) evaluates attributes of the user, data and context, such as department, region or a column's PII tag, so policies scale without creating a role for every combination. Row-level security filters rows per user, such as sales managers seeing only their region, and column-level security or masking hides sensitive columns. Follow least privilege, grant through groups from the identity provider, use tag-based policies, and review access regularly.",
  "One sales table serves all regions: a row access policy filters rows by the user's region attribute, and a masking policy shows customer phone numbers only to the support role.",
  "When does ABAC scale better than RBAC?",
  "Expect to design access for a shared dataset, explain RBAC versus ABAC, and how row and column security work in a warehouse.",
  ["governance", "pii-handling", "snowflake"]),
concept("privacy-regulation", "ar", "Privacy regulation: India's DPDP Act and GDPR", GS, "I",
  "privacy regulation dpdp act digital personal data protection act 2023 dpdp rules 2025 gdpr consent data fiduciary data principal purpose limitation erasure breach notification",
  "India's Digital Personal Data Protection (DPDP) Act and Europe's GDPR set rules for collecting and using personal data, which shape how data platforms store, process and delete it.",
  "Under the DPDP Act 2023, organisations deciding why and how personal data is processed are data fiduciaries, and individuals are data principals. Processing needs valid consent or a permitted legitimate use, limited to the stated purpose; principals can access, correct and erase their data; breaches must be reported to the Data Protection Board and affected people; and penalties can reach Rs 250 crore. The DPDP Rules were notified in November 2025, with obligations phasing in over 12 to 18 months. GDPR has similar principles with more lawful bases and applies to EU residents' data. For platforms this means data inventories, consent records, purpose tags, retention limits, deletion pipelines and breach response.",
  "When a customer withdraws consent for marketing, the consent service publishes an event; pipelines remove them from marketing audiences within 24 hours while keeping order records needed for tax law.",
  "How does a right-to-erasure request affect a data warehouse and its backups?",
  "Expect the key obligations, how you'd implement consent and deletion in a data platform, and differences between DPDP and GDPR.",
  ["pii-handling", "governance", "retention-archival"]),
concept("retention-archival", "ar", "Data retention and archival", GS, "I",
  "data retention archival retention policy legal hold storage tiers glacier archive deletion lifecycle rules purge compliance cold storage",
  "Retention policies decide how long each kind of data is kept and where, balancing legal duties, business value, privacy and cost.",
  "Define retention per data category: for example, tax and invoice records kept for the years tax law requires, logs for months, and marketing data only while consent and purpose last. Move ageing data through storage tiers, from hot to infrequent access to archive classes such as S3 Glacier, using lifecycle rules. Deletion must be real: purge from tables, snapshots and time travel, and let backups expire on schedule. Legal holds pause deletion for data involved in disputes. Document the policy, automate it, and keep evidence that it runs.",
  "Raw clickstream older than 13 months moves to an archive tier and is deleted after 25 months, while GST invoice data stays in the warehouse for eight years, as the finance policy requires.",
  "Why does deleting rows from a table not necessarily remove the data?",
  "Expect to design a retention policy, use storage tiers, and handle deletion and legal holds.",
  ["privacy-regulation", "object-storage", "cost-optimisation"]),
concept("enterprise-dq", "ar", "Enterprise data quality frameworks", GS, "A",
  "enterprise data quality framework dimensions accuracy completeness timeliness consistency validity uniqueness scorecards ownership issue management dq rules",
  "An enterprise data quality framework defines how quality is measured, owned and improved across the organisation, rather than leaving it to individual pipelines.",
  "It starts with quality dimensions: completeness, validity, accuracy, consistency, timeliness and uniqueness. Critical data elements, such as customer id or invoice amount, get rules for each relevant dimension, thresholds and a business owner. Rules run automatically at ingestion and in the warehouse, results feed scorecards by domain, and failures create issues that are triaged, assigned and tracked to root-cause fixes, ideally at the source. Governance bodies prioritise improvements by business impact. Tools range from dbt tests and Great Expectations to enterprise suites such as Informatica Data Quality.",
  "The supplier master scorecard shows GSTIN validity at 92% against a 99% target. Root cause analysis finds a free-text field in the vendor onboarding form, which is replaced with a validated input.",
  "Why should data quality issues be fixed at the source where possible?",
  "Senior governance roles ask you to design a quality framework: dimensions, rules, ownership, scorecards and issue management.",
  ["data-quality", "governance", "mdm"]),
concept("integration-patterns", "ar", "Integration patterns: batch, API, events and files", IA, "F",
  "integration patterns batch file transfer sftp api rest synchronous asynchronous events messaging cdc point to point latency coupling",
  "Systems exchange data in four main ways: scheduled batch or file transfers, synchronous APIs, asynchronous events and messaging, and database change capture.",
  "Batch and file transfers, such as CSV or EDI files over SFTP, are simple and robust for large volumes where hours of delay are acceptable. Synchronous APIs give an immediate answer, such as checking stock, but couple the caller to the provider's availability. Asynchronous events and messaging decouple systems: producers publish and consumers react when ready, which suits order and shipment updates. Change data capture streams database changes without touching the application. The choice depends on latency, volume, coupling, reliability needs and what each system supports. Avoid a tangle of point-to-point integrations by using shared platforms.",
  "An ERP sends nightly item master files to the TMS, the TMS calls a carrier rating API synchronously during load planning, and shipment status events flow asynchronously to customer notifications.",
  "When would you choose an asynchronous event over a synchronous API call?",
  "Expect to choose a pattern for a scenario and explain the trade-offs in latency, coupling and reliability.",
  ["api-ingestion", "file-ingestion", "cdc"]),
concept("event-driven", "ar", "Event-driven architecture", IA, "I",
  "event driven architecture events publish subscribe pub sub event broker kafka event sourcing choreography orchestration saga idempotent consumers decoupling",
  "In an event-driven architecture, services announce facts as events, such as OrderPlaced, and other services react independently, which decouples systems.",
  "Producers publish events to a broker such as Kafka, and any number of consumers subscribe. Events describe something that happened, carry a unique id and timestamp, and follow a versioned schema. Choreography lets services react to each other's events, while orchestration uses a central workflow; long-running processes across services use sagas with compensating actions instead of distributed transactions. Event sourcing stores state as the sequence of events. Benefits include loose coupling, scalability and a built-in history; challenges include eventual consistency, debugging flows across services, ordering, duplicates and schema governance.",
  "When ShipmentDelivered is published, the billing service raises an invoice, the notification service messages the customer, and analytics records the delivery time, without the TMS knowing about any of them.",
  "Why must event consumers be idempotent?",
  "Expect to explain events versus commands, choreography versus orchestration, and how you handle duplicates and ordering.",
  ["kafka-basics", "delivery-semantics", "schema-registry"]),
concept("data-apis", "ar", "Designing APIs for data products", IA, "I",
  "data apis data api design api design rest graphql pagination filtering rate limiting versioning authentication oauth caching sla data products openapi",
  "Data APIs give applications and partners controlled, real-time access to data products, so they need careful design for performance, security and change.",
  "Design resources around consumer needs, such as /shipments/{id}/events, with filtering, sorting and pagination (cursor-based for large or changing data). Serve from a store built for low-latency lookups, such as a cache or operational database fed by pipelines, not from heavy warehouse queries. Secure with OAuth or API keys and scopes, apply rate limits, and document with OpenAPI. Version the API so changes don't break consumers, and publish an SLA for latency and availability. GraphQL lets clients choose fields, which suits varied consumers but needs query cost limits.",
  "A carrier visibility API serves shipment ETAs from a Redis cache updated every minute by a streaming job, with cursor pagination, partner-specific API keys and 100 requests a minute per partner.",
  "Why serve a data API from a cache or operational store rather than directly from the warehouse?",
  "Expect REST design choices, pagination, security, versioning, and where the API's data should come from.",
  ["api-ingestion", "data-contracts", "data-slas"]),
concept("ipaas-esb", "ar", "Enterprise integration: ESB and iPaaS", IA, "I",
  "enterprise service bus esb ipaas integration platform mulesoft boomi informatica cloud azure integration services sap pi po cpi connectors mapping orchestration",
  "Enterprise service buses and integration platforms as a service (iPaaS) connect many business applications through central routing, transformation and connectors.",
  "An ESB, such as older SAP PI/PO or IBM Integration Bus, sits in the middle of the enterprise, routing messages between systems and transforming formats, often on-premises. iPaaS products such as MuleSoft, Boomi, Informatica Cloud, SAP Integration Suite and Azure Integration Services provide this as a cloud service, with prebuilt connectors to SaaS and ERP systems, visual mappings, API management and monitoring. They suit application-to-application integration, such as syncing orders between ERP, TMS and WMS. Analytical data movement at volume usually belongs in data pipelines instead. Govern reusable APIs and mappings to avoid recreating point-to-point spaghetti inside the platform.",
  "MuleSoft exposes an order API from SAP and routes order events to the WMS and TMS, mapping SAP IDocs to each system's JSON format, while bulk history for analytics goes through Data Factory.",
  "Why isn't an iPaaS usually the right tool for loading large volumes into a data warehouse?",
  "Integration architecture roles ask when to use an ESB or iPaaS, how it differs from data pipelines, and common enterprise patterns.",
  ["integration-patterns", "edi-carriers", "informatica"]),
concept("canonical-models", "ar", "Canonical data models", IA, "I",
  "canonical data model common data model standard message format integration mapping translation reduce point to point mappings industry standards oagis gs1",
  "A canonical data model defines a shared, system-neutral format for key business objects, so each system maps to the canonical form once instead of to every other system.",
  "With many systems exchanging orders or shipments, point-to-point mappings grow with the square of the number of systems. A canonical model, such as a standard Shipment message with agreed fields, codes and units, means each system needs only one mapping to and from it. It can borrow from industry standards such as GS1, OAGIS or EDI transaction sets. The risks are designing an over-complicated universal model nobody fully uses, and slow change through central governance. Keep it focused on shared core attributes, version it, allow extensions, and evolve it with the teams that use it.",
  "Five carriers, a TMS, a WMS and an ERP exchange shipment data through one canonical Shipment v2 message, so onboarding a sixth carrier means writing one mapping instead of three.",
  "How does a canonical model reduce the number of mappings between systems?",
  "Expect to explain why canonical models help, their risks, and how you'd govern and version one.",
  ["integration-patterns", "edi-carriers", "data-contracts"]),
concept("nfrs", "ar", "Non-functional requirements for data platforms", DD, "I",
  "non functional requirements nfrs performance scalability availability reliability security latency freshness recoverability rpo rto maintainability cost",
  "Non-functional requirements describe how well a data platform must work, such as how fast, fresh, available, secure and recoverable, and they drive most architecture decisions.",
  "Typical data platform NFRs: data freshness (sales data by 7:00 am), query performance (dashboards under 3 seconds at 200 concurrent users), throughput and scalability (10 million events an hour, growing 50% a year), availability, recoverability (RPO and RTO), security and compliance (encryption, access audit, data residency), maintainability and observability, and cost limits. Each must be measurable and agreed with stakeholders, then traced to design choices and tested before go-live. Vague NFRs like \"fast\" or \"scalable\" can't be designed or tested against.",
  "\"Dashboards should be fast\" becomes \"95% of dashboard queries return within 3 seconds with 200 concurrent users on month-end data volumes\", which drives the choice of an aggregate layer and BI caching.",
  "Why must non-functional requirements be measurable rather than words like fast?",
  "Expect to list NFRs for a platform, make them measurable, and show how they change the design.",
  ["data-slas", "dr-ha", "solution-design-docs"]),
concept("finops", "ar", "Cost architecture and FinOps", DD, "A",
  "finops cost architecture unit cost showback chargeback tagging budgets forecasting reserved capacity commitments cost per query cost governance",
  "FinOps brings financial accountability to cloud spend: engineering, finance and business teams share responsibility for cost, measured against the value delivered.",
  "Cost architecture designs for cost from the start: choosing pricing models (on-demand versus committed capacity), storage tiers, compute isolation and auto-scaling. FinOps runs in cycles of inform, optimise and operate. Inform: tag resources and allocate every cost to a team, product or pipeline, with showback or chargeback. Optimise: right-size, remove waste and commit to discounts where usage is steady. Operate: budgets, alerts, forecasts and regular reviews. Unit economics, such as cost per dashboard query, per 1,000 events or per customer, show whether spend tracks value as the business grows.",
  "Tagging every Databricks job by team showed that one experimental ML team drove 38% of compute. Showback reports and a budget alert halved its spend in two months without slowing other teams.",
  "Why are unit costs more useful than the total monthly bill?",
  "Senior architecture interviews ask how you'd design for cost, allocate spend to teams, and run an ongoing FinOps practice.",
  ["cost-optimisation", "storage-compute", "nfrs"]),
concept("adrs", "ar", "Architecture decision records", DD, "F",
  "architecture decision records adr decision log context decision consequences alternatives trade offs status superseded markdown git",
  "An architecture decision record (ADR) is a short document capturing one significant decision, its context, the options considered and the consequences, so future teams understand why.",
  "Each ADR covers a single decision, such as \"Use Iceberg as the table format\", and has a title, status (proposed, accepted, superseded), context (the problem and constraints), options considered with their trade-offs, the decision, and its consequences, good and bad. ADRs are numbered, kept in Git next to the code or in a shared space, and never edited after acceptance; a new ADR supersedes an old one. They're short, often one page, and written when the decision is made. They prevent reopening settled debates and help new team members.",
  "ADR-014: \"Use dbt for warehouse transformations.\" Context: 300 stored procedures with no tests. Options: keep procedures, Dataform, dbt. Decision: dbt. Consequences: retraining analysts, a migration of six months.",
  "Why are accepted ADRs superseded by new ones rather than edited?",
  "Expect to explain what goes in an ADR and how you'd use them to document architecture decisions on a project.",
  ["solution-design-docs", "git-for-data", "nfrs"]),
concept("migrations", "ar", "Legacy-to-cloud migration strategies", DD, "A",
  "migration strategies legacy to cloud lift and shift rehost replatform refactor rebuild retire 6 rs teradata hadoop oracle parallel run reconciliation cutover waves",
  "Moving a legacy data platform, such as Teradata, Oracle, Hadoop or on-premises ETL, to the cloud needs a strategy per workload, careful validation and a staged cutover.",
  "Start with discovery: inventory sources, pipelines, reports, dependencies, usage and data volumes, and retire what nobody uses. Choose a strategy per workload using the classic options: rehost (lift and shift), replatform (move with small changes, such as to a cloud warehouse), refactor or rebuild (redesign, for example into ELT with dbt), and retire. Automated code conversion tools help with SQL and ETL translation. Migrate in waves by domain, run old and new in parallel, and reconcile results with row counts, totals and sample comparisons. Plan the cutover, consumer communication and decommissioning, and avoid moving old problems unchanged.",
  "A Teradata warehouse migrates to Snowflake in four waves by domain. Each wave runs in parallel for two month-ends, with automated reconciliation of 1,200 report totals before users switch.",
  "Why run old and new platforms in parallel before cutover?",
  "Senior architecture interviews ask you to plan a migration: discovery, strategy per workload, validation, cutover and risks.",
  ["informatica", "cloud-platforms", "testing-pipelines"]),
concept("dr-ha", "ar", "High availability and disaster recovery", DD, "A",
  "high availability disaster recovery rpo rto recovery point objective recovery time objective replication multi region backups failover availability zones runbook",
  "High availability keeps a platform running through component failures, while disaster recovery restores it after a major outage, within agreed recovery targets.",
  "The recovery point objective (RPO) is how much data you can afford to lose, measured in time, and the recovery time objective (RTO) is how long recovery may take. High availability comes from redundancy within a region: multiple availability zones, managed services with built-in replication, and retries. Disaster recovery covers losing a whole region or a corruption event, using cross-region replication, backups and immutable snapshots, and infrastructure as code to rebuild quickly. Tighter RPO and RTO cost more, so tier datasets by criticality. Document runbooks and test failover regularly, because untested DR plans usually fail.",
  "The payments ledger replicates to a second region with RPO 5 minutes and RTO 1 hour, while the marketing mart is rebuilt from the lake within a day, as agreed for its tier.",
  "What's the difference between RPO and RTO?",
  "Expect to define RPO and RTO, design HA and DR for a data platform, and explain how you'd test it.",
  ["nfrs", "iac-terraform", "object-storage"]),
concept("solution-design-docs", "ar", "Writing solution designs and SOWs", DD, "I",
  "solution design document sow statement of work scope deliverables assumptions dependencies risks architecture diagrams acceptance criteria estimates out of scope",
  "A solution design document explains how a system will meet its requirements, and a statement of work (SOW) defines what will be delivered, by when and on what terms.",
  "A solution design covers the business context and requirements, functional and non-functional, the target architecture with diagrams, data flows and integrations, key design decisions with alternatives, security, operations, risks and open questions. A SOW turns it into a commercial commitment: objectives, scope and explicit out-of-scope items, deliverables with acceptance criteria, milestones and timeline, roles and responsibilities on both sides, assumptions and dependencies, estimates and pricing basis, and change control. Ambiguity in scope and assumptions causes most project disputes, so make them specific and agreed.",
  "A TMS integration SOW lists 6 interfaces in scope, states that carrier onboarding beyond the first 10 carriers is out of scope, assumes the client provides EDI test partners by week 3, and defines acceptance as 2 weeks of error-free parallel runs.",
  "Why are explicit out-of-scope items and assumptions so important in a SOW?",
  "Consulting and architect roles ask how you structure a solution design or SOW and how you manage scope and assumptions.",
  ["adrs", "nfrs", "tms-data-model"]),
]

